#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:6
msgid "Continuous Documentation Deployment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:8
msgid ""
"Read the Docs is a *Continuous Documentation Deployment* platform for "
"your software project. Every time you change something in your "
"documentation, Read the Docs will detect your change and build your "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:11
msgid ""
"The Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) features are"
" configured with your repository provider, such as GitHub, Bitbucket or "
"GitLab. With each change committed to your repository, we are notified by"
" the configured *webhook*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:15
msgid ""
"When we receive a *webhook*, we match it to a project's *Integration*, "
"and the following steps happen:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:18
msgid ":doc:`Build </builds>` the latest commit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:19
msgid ""
"Synchronize your :doc:`versions </versions>` based on the latest tag and "
"branch data in Git."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:20
msgid "Run your :doc:`automation rules</automation-rules>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:21
msgid "Auto-cancel any currently running builds of the same version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:22
msgid "Add a log entry to the integration's :guilabel:`Recent Activity`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:25
msgid "Documentation as code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:27
msgid ""
"Documentation fits into any CI/CD pipeline by following a process known "
"as *Documentation as Code (Docs as code)*. This means that the "
"documentation's life cycle is the same as your software project. By "
"managing these changes in the same life cycle, you can benefit from "
"**documentation and source code being part of the same code review "
"process**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:32
msgid ""
"Having an automated and short feedback loop for your documentation allows"
" you to keep it updated with minimal effort. This allows more iteration "
"on documentation, and increases overall value from the documentation you "
"write."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:37
msgid ""
"As part of this quick feedback loop, you can preview documentation "
"changes immediately using :doc:`pull request previews </pull-requests>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:41
msgid "Automated versioning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:43
msgid ""
"With Read the Docs you will be able to fully align your project's "
"**release cycle** with your documentation. For instance, a new version of"
" a software project can build and publish a :doc:`new documentation "
"version </versions>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:46
msgid ""
"When you release a new version for your project, you are likely also "
"adding a version tag to your Git repository. These Git events can be "
"configured to build and publish your documentation automatically with "
":doc:`/automation-rules`. If you use a versioning scheme, you can "
"configure it as part of the automation process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:51
msgid ""
"Whether you choose to handle versioning automatically or with manual "
"control is up to you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/continuous-deployment.rst:53
msgid ""
"Read the Docs will store your version history and make it possible for "
"users to visit archived versions of your documentation. Your version "
"setup is ultimately captured by the :term:`flyout menu`, and can be "
"integrated in your documentation with :doc:`/addons`."
msgstr ""

